From 8d9d84f67e101cf15be0f47dac5571e784a6c2a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 19:28:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] feat(storage): implement AnyRAID on LVM and remove the
 phantom CSI driver

installer/manifests/30-anyraid-csi.yaml declared a CSIDriver plus a DaemonSet
running ghcr.io/sggr57a/nexus-anyraid-csi:1.0.0. That image is never built:
no source and no build recipe for it exists anywhere in the repository, and
the only Dockerfile is the ISO builder. On a real install the DaemonSet went
to ImagePullBackOff and every PVC on the anyraid-default StorageClass stayed
Pending indefinitely, so AnyRAID was advertised but non-functional.

Implement it over LVM instead of writing a new block layer. LVM already
provides the primitive AnyRAID describes: a volume group pools physical
volumes of differing sizes and allocates in fixed-size extents (the 'slabs'),
and its RAID targets sit on dm-raid, the same kernel code mdadm drives.
Reimplementing a slab allocator would duplicate well-tested storage code
where bugs destroy data.

Profiles map onto dm-raid levels: mirror->raid1, striped-mirror->raid10,
raidz1->raid5, raidz2->raid6. raidz3 is rejected rather than silently
downgraded, because dm-raid has no triple-parity target and delivering
2-drive tolerance for a 3-parity request would misrepresent redundancy.

plan_pool reports stranded capacity per drive, since equal-leg layouts are
bounded by the smallest member and a raw-sum total would over-promise.
Device paths are validated against a strict pattern and every call uses
argument lists, so wizard input cannot smuggle flags or escape /dev.

Pool health is served from GET /api/v1/storage/anyraid by the cockpit, which
already runs on the host, rather than by a privileged hostPID DaemonSet.

Verified on real heterogeneous loop devices (640M/640M/1.12G/1.94G):
pvcreate and vgcreate created a genuine 4-PV volume group; lvcreate is
unverified here only because this container kernel exposes no device-mapper
target. Planning, validation, and capacity accounting are covered by 16 new
tests (312 -> 328).
